#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32fc8440-e872-50e4-ab98-5861d1c7310e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "0350faac-e5c6-56dc-af12-3c90e0c16bf4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5378" :question/problem "Given that the asymptotes of the hyperbola below are $y=\\pm\\dfrac{5}{7}x,$ find the parametric equations of the hyperbola.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/fd2618e961c15a9b0665e73e18544ab08b0d611b962bc3771558c61450cf49e5.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(\\pm a, 0).$ From the graph, we have that $(7,0)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=7.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a horizontal hyperbola are $y=\\pm \\dfrac{b}{a}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y=\\pm\\dfrac57x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{b}{a} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ \\frac{b}{7} & =\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=5\\tan{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087492 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "961ff66e-ce82-5ef7-bd5e-d5aa14c1ec58" :question/math-academy-id "e-5379" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola with the parametric equations $x = 6\\sec{t},\\, y = 4 \\sqrt{2}\\tan{t}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec t$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{6})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{2}})}^{2} & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(4\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{32}+1 & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{32} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/id #uuid "0fc71252-9b97-560d-be87-74b36d515823" :question/math-academy-id "e-7883" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\displaystyle\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} - \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\sec{t},\\quad y=b\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{49} = 7$ and $b =\\sqrt9= 3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=7\\sec{t},\\quad y=3\\tan{t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "5c66fba2-b645-543a-8417-bbc246db55e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7896" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6e12d114aedfd6c5c8ab5830e052fcc1dd3c937b825665a0490b42ca0c87972c.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/730325137cfca58eb17d65103e539d084f81d7ab810628261125c29dbb8a0284.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right)$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{\\pi}2\\right).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2669e657-da47-5e22-9ac5-7a69f3b0a55f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Horizontal Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c3c6d74-b01d-5edf-b002-4b32b47fe7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331442}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c3c6d74-b01d-5edf-b002-4b32b47fe7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\tan(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331442} :answer-field/id #uuid "39482ac3-275e-5c64-9ecd-bfd6084b5347" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c609c76a-8513-596f-b37c-531435907db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325901" :question/problem "Complete the following parametric equations generating the hyperbola $2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2}=16$ in terms of the parameter $t,$ defined for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ where $t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n$x=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,$\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal hyperbola with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation of our hyperbola in standard form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-8{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{2{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{8{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}$ and $b=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e79ab75-6bf0-51df-b198-33b5f76831cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331472} {:answer/id #uuid "14140dcb-a619-5447-bdc3-acc8ad253aad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e79ab75-6bf0-51df-b198-33b5f76831cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331472} :answer-field/id #uuid "feb3c842-a366-5fa5-b0eb-b3e573c27efb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87825b4c-9345-5473-8ba0-aee302e06a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87825b4c-9345-5473-8ba0-aee302e06a27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331475} :answer-field/id #uuid "173700aa-587b-5075-ba28-7abe8c0039d8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331474}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f76d65f-5129-5d16-94bc-f52d8a4e4d7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-325985" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/5b133ac885861d82704a6d6c2d99b8e4179210e4f28e94bc6d7283da23f97042.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDetermine the domain of the parameter $t$ for this quarter, assuming that the entire parabola is defined only for $t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$\n\n$t\\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14053984/assets/6ac31692404208d80637291fb30be028a7efdc3a172af0337da26039f1e87326.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6ebe1f1-40fe-5226-8aed-b321f2e1e58b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be4e5f83-e674-56a1-b13e-f3bd71eb9030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{5} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086851} {:answer/id #uuid "e85ad4fd-12cb-51a5-9dc1-2f277fe28ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^{2}}{5} - \\frac{x^{2}}{9} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086852} {:answer/id #uuid "2489233b-4ca4-5618-a725-c75e0365e05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} - \\frac{x^{2}}{3} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086853} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3f9ecc-fdf5-5643-a37d-1c4284ae5c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{9} - \\frac{y^{2}}{5} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086854} {:answer/id #uuid "6a5b949b-e70b-5645-85cb-7bc1cc260fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{\\sqrt{5}} - \\frac{y^{2}}{3} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be4e5f83-e674-56a1-b13e-f3bd71eb9030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{2}}{5} - \\frac{y^{2}}{9} = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086851} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae8a271-74be-5cca-9b44-4eba4bd10b9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086850}] :question/id #uuid "8b759c31-90c7-5fab-8980-94a67e08f99a" :question/math-academy-id "q-7987" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x = \\sqrt{5}\\sec t, y = 3\\tan t$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8c8c92a-aa84-5387-89cc-ffd443c248d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087283} {:answer/id #uuid "e5211b55-578f-5cde-873b-d12b5b12e6a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (- π, 0), t \\ne - \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087284} {:answer/id #uuid "f1d0ffdc-4f9e-5bc4-858a-48c1aeb794b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [0, π], t \\ne \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087285} {:answer/id #uuid "50373bf6-f341-5011-817a-24c5a4de90d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})∪(\\frac{π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087286} {:answer/id #uuid "75564cf1-9da6-5201-b6e3-02ed66af31eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (0, π), t \\ne \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8c8c92a-aa84-5387-89cc-ffd443c248d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087283} :answer-field/id #uuid "84e17c5e-1f92-59fc-abf0-af1363d0c53b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087282}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9ce8075-169d-58ab-857b-a9a6feb55ea5" :question/math-academy-id "q-89345" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/90b505954bb714ad86f60e07f7a26c16c954139689d96684ed553a7b494f0527.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14064533/assets/b43dcf6c343994e3226e2840a0ae80f60484c8de5d7984f61ec6447185181a76.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]$ and $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the right branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the right branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=7\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14527450-9b34-5982-9d87-b5a31870a70e" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Horizontal Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38bc38bc-4117-5713-bcc7-f41bb36fdc4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335856} {:answer/id #uuid "fcdc42b4-c773-5263-b22c-7ab06b99321c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335857} {:answer/id #uuid "8a68e4a6-0103-5b47-bebc-81ac24dd3af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\cup (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335858} {:answer/id #uuid "6240f72a-0d02-5d66-bf9c-695aad894866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335859} {:answer/id #uuid "643a4bb6-ecd5-5d11-bb9b-2e1773973ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fcdc42b4-c773-5263-b22c-7ab06b99321c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335857} :answer-field/id #uuid "61d234ec-66e7-50ac-b720-163a2cf9d3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "698299c1-e40e-528b-a38d-66f6a77ea0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-89347" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/a2f0f570ecc68de1a5b34fb3bd1b341fc83a728b026b035dde848dbf986e441b.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14058380/assets/e7ef7fd90c828767359daf69f68f406d61e3f82ad86b1f6b3a9480c99dedadf8.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0],$ we get the quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0].\n$$"}]}
